---
/** Introduction: what Satin is, who it is for and how to use it, on one page. */
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import Chip from '../components/Chip.astro'
import { page } from '../nav'
import CodeFrame from '../components/CodeFrame.astro'

const componentsJson = `{
  "$schema": "https://ui.shadcn.com/schema.json",
  "style": "new-york",
  "rsc": false,
  "tsx": true,
  "tailwind": {
    "config": "",
    "css": "",
    "baseColor": "neutral",
    "cssVariables": true,
    "prefix": ""
  },
  "aliases": {
    "components": "@/components",
    "ui": "@/components/ui",
    "lib": "@/lib",
    "utils": "@/lib/utils",
    "hooks": "@/hooks"
  }
}`
const alias = `// tsconfig.json (and tsconfig.app.json in Vite apps)
"compilerOptions": {
  "paths": { "@/*": ["./src/*"] }
}

// vite.config.ts
resolve: { alias: { '@': path.resolve(import.meta.dirname, 'src') } }`
const registryUsage = `// main.tsx, once for the whole app
import './styles/satin.css'

// App.tsx
import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button'

export function Save() {
  return <Button>Save changes</Button>
}`
const packageUsage = `// main.tsx, once for the whole app
import '@satin/ui/styles.css'

// App.tsx
import { Button } from '@satin/ui/button'

export function Save() {
  return <Button>Save changes</Button>
}`
const h2 = 'text-title font-medium text-text-default'
const h3 = 'text-body-lg leading-6 font-medium text-text-default'
const prose = 'text-body-lg leading-6 text-text-muted'
const link = 'rounded-sm text-text-default underline underline-offset-2 focus-visible:shadow-border-focus focus-visible:outline-none'
---

<Docs current="introduction">
  <PageHeader id="introduction" title={page('introduction').title} description={page('introduction').description} />

  <section aria-labelledby="what-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-4">
    <h2 id="what-title" class={h2}>What is Satin?</h2>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-body-lg leading-6 text-text-muted">
      <p>Satin ships as a Figma library and as React components that match it one to one: same names, same variants, same tokens.</p>
      <p>
        The components are built on <a class="text-text-default underline underline-offset-2" href="https://ui.shadcn.com">shadcn/ui</a> and Radix, so focus, keyboard and screen-reader behaviour come from proven primitives. Satin changes how they look: colour, surfaces, borders, focus and motion all come from Satin tokens.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="who-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-8">
    <h2 id="who-title" class={h2}>Who is Satin for?</h2>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-body-lg leading-6 text-text-muted">
      <li><span class="font-medium text-text-default">Designers</span> who work in the Satin Figma library and want to see how a component behaves before they use it.</li>
      <li><span class="font-medium text-text-default">Engineers</span> who need production code that matches the design without re-creating it by hand.</li>
      <li><span class="font-medium text-text-default">Teams building AI products</span> who need ready answers for prompts, agent progress and decisions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="how-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-8">
    <h2 id="how-title" class={h2}>How can I use Satin?</h2>
    <p class={prose}>
      Two ways, and neither needs Tailwind: Satin ships its tokens and every class the components use as one precompiled stylesheet. Copy the components you need into your app with the shadcn CLI and own the code, or install the package and import them. Light and dark mode come from the <Chip>dark</Chip> class on any parent element.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="installation" aria-labelledby="installation-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-8">
    <h2 id="installation-title" class={h2}>Installation</h2>
    <p class={prose}>Satin needs React 19 or newer. Fonts: the tokens ask for Geist and Geist Mono; load them, or the system font is used.</p>

    <h3 class:list={[h3, 'pt-3']}>Copy components with the shadcn CLI</h3>
    <p class={prose}>
      Every component is a <a class={link} href="https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/registry">shadcn registry</a> item. The CLI copies its source into <Chip>components/ui</Chip>, with the components it builds on, the <Chip>cn</Chip> helper and Satin’s precompiled styles, and installs its npm dependencies. Set your project up once:
    </p>
    <ol class="flex list-decimal flex-col gap-3 pl-5 text-body-lg leading-6 text-text-muted marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li>
        <p class="pb-3">Add the <Chip>@/</Chip> import alias, pointing at <Chip>src</Chip>.</p>
        <CodeFrame code={alias} label="Import alias" />
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="pb-3">
          Add a <Chip>components.json</Chip> at the root of the project. Write it by hand: <Chip>shadcn init</Chip> stops when it can’t find Tailwind, and Satin doesn’t need it, so the Tailwind paths stay empty.
        </p>
        <CodeFrame code={componentsJson} label="components.json" lang="json" />
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="pb-3">Add components by URL. Each component page has its own command under Installation.</p>
        <CodeFrame code="npx shadcn@latest add https://uisatin.dev/r/button.json" label="Terminal" lang="bash" copyLabel="Copy command" />
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="pb-3">Import the styles once, then use the components.</p>
        <CodeFrame code={registryUsage} label="Usage" />
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class:list={[h3, 'pt-6']} id="npm">Install the npm package</h3>
    <p class={prose}>
      <Chip>@satin/ui</Chip> ships compiled ES modules with types and <Chip>styles.css</Chip>. Every component has its own entry point, so you only ship what you use. Until it is published, build it from the Satin repository with <Chip>npm run build:ui</Chip> and install the <Chip>packages/ui/dist</Chip> folder.
    </p>
    <CodeFrame code="npm i @satin/ui" label="Terminal" lang="bash" copyLabel="Copy command" />
    <CodeFrame code={packageUsage} label="Usage" />
  </section>
</Docs>
